.TH XARGS 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
xargs \- build and run command lines from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xargs
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RI [ \-E " eofstr" ]
.RI [ \-I " replstr" ]
.RI [ \-L " number" ]
.RI [ \-n " number" ]
.RI [ \-s " size" ]
.RI [ utility " [" argument\ .\|.\|. ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xargs
reads whitespace/quote-delimited arguments (see INPUT GRAMMAR below)
from standard input, and invokes
.I utility
.RB ( echo ,
if none is given) one or more times, each invocation's argument list
built from
.I utility
and any
.I argument
operands given, followed by as many of the input-derived arguments as
fit within the active batching rule and a real command-line length
limit, until standard input is exhausted.
.PP
Each invocation's own exit status is tracked, but invocations continue
regardless of any individual one's failure, with one exception: under
.B \-I
(see OPTIONS below), no further invocation is attempted once a
previous one could not be found or could not be spawned at all; the
default and
.B \-L
batching forms do not apply that same cutoff, and keep issuing
invocations regardless of any previous invocation's exit status.
.SS Input grammar
Arguments are delimited from one another by any run of unquoted
<blank> or <newline> characters. A run of zero or more bytes that are
neither a double-quote character nor a newline can be quoted by
enclosing it in a pair of double-quote characters; a run of zero or
more bytes that are not a single-quote character can similarly be
quoted by enclosing it in a pair of single-quote characters. Outside
of either kind of quoting, any single character (including a
<blank>, a newline, or a quote character) can be made literal by
preceding it with a backslash. A backslash has no special meaning
.I inside
either kind of quoting \(em quoting and backslash-escaping are two
independent mechanisms here, never nested the way shell quoting is.
.SS Batching
Exactly one of
.BR \-I ", " \-L ,
or the default token/\-n/\-s-driven batching described under OPTIONS
is in effect for the whole run;
.B \-I
implies
.B "\-L 1"
and
.BR \-x .
.PP
Every invocation's combined argument and environment size is kept
under a byte ceiling: the value given to
.BR \-s ,
if any (otherwise a compiled-in default), but never more than a
ceiling derived from the system's own real argument-list-size limit
minus the current environment's size \(em so
.B \-s
can request a stricter limit than that derived ceiling, but never a
looser one. If a single argument alone would already exceed the
ceiling, an error is reported and no further invocations are
attempted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
Before each invocation (after tracing it, as
.B \-t
would), ask whether to run it; a response read from standard input
that begins, after any leading blanks, with
.B y
or
.B Y
runs it, anything else skips it. Implies the same tracing
.B \-t
causes.
.TP
.B \-t
Write each invocation's argument list, space-separated, to standard
error just before running it.
.TP
.B \-x
If a batch built under
.B \-n
could not include all
.I number
arguments
.B \-n
asked for without exceeding the byte ceiling, stop with an error
instead of running a shorter batch. Implied by
.BR \-I .
.TP
.BI \-E " eofstr"
Treat
.I eofstr
as a logical end-of-input marker: parsing of standard input stops at
(and discards) the first token exactly equal to
.IR eofstr ,
as if standard input had ended there. Without
.BR \-E ,
no token is ever treated as an end-of-input marker, regardless of its
value.
.TP
.BI \-I " replstr"
Run
.I utility
once per input line (a run of input that the input grammar above
would otherwise have split into several arguments, up to the next
unquoted newline, is instead rejoined, space-separated, into one
value for this purpose), with every occurrence of
.I replstr
in
.I utility
and every
.I argument
operand replaced by that line's value. Implies
.B "\-L 1"
and
.BR \-x .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-L .
.TP
.BI \-L " number"
Batch input by whole lines: each invocation gets as many complete
input lines' worth of arguments as fit within the byte ceiling, up to
.I number
lines. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-I .
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Put at most
.I number
input-derived arguments in each invocation (still bounded by the byte
ceiling).
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
Use
.I size
bytes, instead of a compiled-in default, as the requested
per-invocation byte ceiling described under Batching above.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I utility
The command to run.
.B echo
is used if none is given.
.TP
.I argument
A fixed leading argument to include in every invocation, before the
input-derived ones; under
.BR \-I ,
instead, a template argument that
.I replstr
is substituted into.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.BR \-0 " and " \-d ,
which would read NUL-delimited or custom-delimited input instead of
the grammar described above, are not implemented; either is refused
with a diagnostic and exit status 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every invocation exited 0.
.TP
1\(en125
At least one invocation exited nonzero (the largest such exit status
actually seen, if it stayed below 126), or an internal error prevented
an invocation from being assembled or attempted.
.TP
.B 126
A utility was found but could not be invoked.
.TP
.B 127
A utility could not be found at all.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR find (1),
.BR grep (1)
